  "question": {
    "text": "If the lines $\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{4}$ and $\\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-k}{2}=\\frac{z}{1}$ intersect, then $k$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "mZUYE",
      "text": "\n$-1$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "uJU2h",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{2}{9}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Wfx1m",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{9}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "0GCkR",
      "text": "\n$0$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Any point on $\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{4}=t$ is $(2 t+1,3 t-1,4 t+1)$ \nAnd any point on $\\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-k}{2}=\\frac{z}{1}=\\mathrm{s}$ is $(s+3,2 s+k, s)$\nGiven lines are intersecting\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{t}=-\\frac{3}{2} \\text { and } \\mathrm{s}=-5 \\quad \\therefore \\mathrm{k}=\\frac{9}{2}$",
    "image": null
  },
